**[W5, Q2] The “lightweight approximation” is a major compromise. Why does it not collapse the theoretical framework? (con't)**

Importantly, prior work in in-context learning has repeatedly shown that the contribution of few-shot examples is highly uneven, and that the most relevant example often carries a disproportionately large fraction of the useful signal [2,3,4]. From this perspective, selecting the most relevant demonstration does not replace the conceptual role of the backward term; it simply offers a tractable proxy that captures most of the signal. Our empirical results support this view: as shown in Table 18 (for convenience, we reproduce this table below), the performance gap between the full backward computation and the lightweight approximation is negligible across benchmarks, even though the lightweight version substantially reduces computation.

