Even with full backward computation, ReFeri Full (1B) achieves stronger accuracy than CoT-WP using an 8B estimator despite requiring similar or lower computation. For example, on MuSR-ta, ReFeri Full (1B) achieves 83.2% accuracy with a cost of 21.3s per query, whereas CoT-WP (8B) achieves 78.8% accuracy with 25.6s. In contrast, CoT-WP experiences substantial degradation when reducing the estimator from 8B to 1B, while ReFeri maintains stable performance across estimator scales.

These findings demonstrate two key points:

1. **The full Bayesian structure remains unchanged and practically usable**: the lightweight version is optional, not foundational.
2. **When used, the lightweight approximation preserves the essential function of the backward term** and does not collapse the theoretical framework, as supported by both the observations in prior works and empirical evidence.

We appreciate the reviewer’s insightful comment. We have added this discussion, along with the new results, to Appendix B.8 of the revised draft and will incorporate it into the camera-ready version.
